Date: Sun, 24 Oct 93 23:11:13 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Beating the Stock Market?

Young Loudmouth writes:
> 	Is what we're talking about overlearning?  As in the NN/GP/whatever
> comes up with something which essentially memorizes the part of the data
> set we're interested in?

Yes.  Another name for this is "overfitting", which comes from
statistics (regression).

> 	Aren't there some fairly standard tricks to at least cut down
> appreciably on memorization by grinding on a "test" subset and checking
> either a larger or a separate chunk of the dataset? 

Yes, the main traditional method is to do an independent post-test:
test the statstical validity of the model on a different set
of data than was used to generate the model.  There are a a few
problems with this.  One problem is that the generator
will often find ephemeral patterns that exist in the sample data but 
not the post-test data.  Even worse, if we use post-test as an
excuse to forget about parsimony, the in-sample data may simply
be "memorized", generating a "brilliant penny" that fails
on the post-test data.  To avoid wasting the vast majority of model 
generator cycles, the generating process itself (whether human with 
a statistics package, GP, NN, whatever) still needs to worry about 
parsimony.  

Yet another problem is that the generator may not find a 
pattern at all, or may underestimate the statistical viability of the 
pattern, because there is not enough data in the sample set.  This is 
especially true when, as is usually the case, the most recent or otherwise 
most significant data is saved for the post-test.   Without the need 
for a post-test we might cut our need for data by half or more, cutting 
in half our time-to-market. (These models quickly become obsolete once
they become widely used.  One of my goals is rapid development
of disposable strategies, finding the patterns and bringing the
corresponding trading strategies to market as soon as the
patterns become statistically significant, and moving on when
the patterns have become exhausted by overtrading or decay
in the underlying pattern).

If the information-theoretic probability of
finding the model that parsiminously explains part of the
data is low enough, we can be confident that we have avoided 
overfitting and don't need to throw out the best part of the
data set to save it for independent verification.  I also
suspect that, since it's measured over the entire available
data, the parsimony-derived risk estimate will be more accurate
than the the risk estimate derived from the post-test on the
out-of-sample data.  (this remains to be mathematically verified).

