I think there are two distinct issues here, though I don't disagree
with the spirit of Nick's use of "brilliant penny" to capture the core
ideas.

First, finding patterns where none "really" exist, as in crunching on
a truly random (coin tosses, radioactive decays, cryptographically
strong pseudorandom number generator) data set and then reaching
"conclusions" (rules, heuristics, periodicities, etc.). Many
"postdictive" analyses fall into this trap.

Second, an investor can become convinced that a system "really works"
by selective exposure of information, as in the "brilliant penny" scam
and any number of other cons and magic tricks. 

Each of these points rates more extensive discussion. 


