Derek Zahn: 
> If you can trade and read both
> markets instantaneously, doesn't that mean that delta-t is zero?

In that simple binary price model Delta-t=1 unit is the amount of 
time for the price change to propagate from one market to the other
when the data was gathered.  One of the things a better 
arbitrage model has to do is collect the data, compute the arbitrage 
and execute it in a time shorter than the current lag time.
The current "arbitrage" might involve a wide variety of indirect
signal propagations, for example market reaction to release of
a financial statement that reflects info already available
before the statement was released, but no volume trader had
made the connection.  Another example is wheat futures
responding to flood news on the radio, but the gauge data was 
available on-line somewhere a few minutes sooner, perhaps
enough to gain a big edge for a trader-bot that could recognize
the weather data's consequences when it saw it.  So the lag might 
be seconds to months, depending on the situation.

> "The amount of information given
> by the realization of two finite schemes A and B equals the
> amount of information given by the realization of scheme A
> plus the mathematical expectation of the amount of additional
> information given by the realization of scheme B after the
> realization of the scheme A."

Plus the amount of information needed to specify that mathematical
relationship.  Or, in general, the size of the Turing machine that
gives us B from A.  This is where AIT the comes in.  In many real world
cases, that model is as complex is the data it's supposed
to represent -- overfit and therefore much more likely to be a "brilliant
penny" reflecting the random details of the data rather than a
significant ongoing pattern.   (The elementary counting argument 
-- most strings are mostly random).

> I suppose that you're driving toward the idea of using
> something like Genetic Programming with a high parsimony
> bias to construct arbitrage models for the two sequences.

And, in addition, using Htm to determine the
probability we have found a real pattern, and incorporating
that into the risk profile of the portfolio alongside the
unhedged random/unmodelled components.  This can be done
both in the fitness function and afterwords to prove the
model has found a real pattern, in lieu of the traditional
post-test on out-of-sample data, which is wasteful.

> My doubt comes from the fact that every trader in the
> market is building models of the market and the actual
> market prices are emergent phenomena of their ensemble.
> I'm not sure that Occam's razor is as compelling in
> such a circumstance.

Occam's Razor is compelling from AIT consideration of the
model and the maximum information available in the data,
regardless of how that information was crammed in there.
We can only hope to find small predictable components.  It's
futile to try to predict most of the price most of the time.
Almost all of the price is going to be random most of
time, reflecting all these other players and information we
don't have.  Fortuneately, much of that randomness (c. 70%) is 
covariant with other securities, eg a basket of stocks in the same 
industry, and can be hedged away.  For stocks, we have to compress 
a small percentage of the remaining 30% during a small fraction
of epochs, eg responding to certain kinds of news, various
econometric data releases, etc. to come up with a strategy that wins 
with high probability after a reasonable number of trades.  

Other securities are better or worse in various ways.  For example fixed 
income securities (bonds, money market, etc.) and their synthetic assets (eg
bonds stripped of their coupons, bond options, floaters and inverse
floaters, etc. etc. etc.) are often deterministic after hedging out 
interest rates and currency fluctuations, the remaining task being to 
find often complex nonlinear relationships between them.  
Fixed-income securities would be ideal for GP except that market 
data is proprietary and scarce.  For commodities it can be 
harder to hedge away unknown information and market randomness, 
but the cause-and-effect between available data (harvest & 
stockpile surveys, weather, etc.) is often clearer than
with hi-tech stocks (although still complex enough I suspect futures 
traders are missing many good complex nonlinear models).

As for sports wagering, there's a guy I met at a GP lunch who
is doing regression from the horse racing form.  His models so far 
beat the average but still lose money (no surprise -- the house takes 
something like 17%).

