Date: Mon, 25 Oct 93 1:41:33 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Beating the Stock Market?

Tim May: 
> [Two kinds of "brilliant penny"]:
> First, finding patterns where none "really" exist, as in crunching on
> a truly random ... data set...

> Second, an investor can become convinced that a system "really works"
> by selective exposure of information,

The reason I don't make a big distinction is that the method of
"crunching" typically involves just such selective exposure of
those models that make the most money (whether the crunching
involves a human picking rules, algorithms, regression models,
etc. or a learning algorithm searching the space of models).

Specifically, for GP to work well, it needs an automated way of rejecting 
such brilliant pennies.  Htm/(H(x)-H(residual)) not only measures 
the parsimony, it also, by the string enumeration/counting argument,
measures the probability that H(x)-H(residual) is random, ie the
probability that the model is a brilliant penny.  This allows
GP to compute, in every fitness case, the riskiness of the resulting 
portfolio, based not only on the randomness of the unhedged residuals, 
but also on the probability that the model itself will work.  

Currently the "algorithmic trading" community uses a lot of messy rules 
of thumb to incorporate parsimony into rather subjective estimates of 
the reliability of the strategy, often loosely based on measures like 
the number of independent variables, but often ignoring the amount of
data the model attempts to explain.  This often done manually or 
post-hoc, not as an automated part of the model generation process, 
as I am proposing here.  There is nothing in use resembling a rigorous 
information-theoretic comparison of the model and the data it 
compresses.

