Date: Mon, 25 Oct 1993 10:43:19 -0700
From: thamilto@pcocd2.intel.com (Tony Hamilton - FES ERG~)
Subject: Beating the Stock Market?

On Oct 24, 11:16am, Derek Zahn wrote:
> Subject: Beating the Stock Market?
> Tony Hamilton:
>
> > Don't be so sure, Ray (on the MS and NT thing). Early indicators show NT
> > losing momentum in a big way to OS/2.
>
> Could you elaborate on this a bit?  What are these early indicators?
> As a software developer I'm very curious.  My personal analysis has
> been that the Windows line will win:

Be glad to elaborate. Point by point...

> * Windows 3.1:  Huge and growing installed base.  More software
> for 3.1 is being sold than for any other "operating system".
> Tens of millions of people run it.  This is important because,
> come upgrade time, people often take the path of least resistance
> unless there are overwhelming reasons to learn a new environment.

Win #1 for OS/2 v2.1: Runs Win3.1 software faster than win3.1! Also runs and
multitasks DOS applications much better than Windows 3.1. But yes, the ONLY
advantage Windows 3.1 currently has is a much larger installed base. The
point is, however, that 32-bit OS's are the new battleground. THe
assumption has always been that the 32-bit follow-on to Windows, because
of installed base, will by default be the winner. The tricky part, however,
is that OS/2 _is_ a valid Windows 3.1 follow-on. Let us not forget that IBM
is fully licensed to use any Windows 3.1 _OR_ Windows NT technology. That
agreement came to end, of course, in September. But it means that the
validity of anything IBM makes to follow on to 3.1 or NT is just as sound
as anything Microsoft makes. Truly, the best OS will win, not just the one
with the name "Microsoft" on it.

> * Windows 4 (Chicago):  NT for the masses.  Looks to be a clean
> and consistent upgrade path.

Actually, not at all NT for the masses. Chicago is actually far more
sophisticated than NT, just not as powerful as all. It is a completely
separate path, however, from NT. There are two paths. One is the Windows
3.1 to Windows 4.0 path. The other is NT to Cairo. Note that I didn't
mention a Windows 3.1 to NT path. Why? Because it virtually doesn't exist!
Will talk about that more later, though.

A couple of key points: It is rumored (I think rumor, perhaps an actual
announcement or statement was made) that there are some problems with
Chicago (Win4.0). This news comes after MS announced the intention to step
up development. Originaly plan was to release in Q1 of 94. Now we may be
looking at end of next year.

Another point is the API controversy. There used to be Win32, Win32s, Win32c,
and so forth and so on. The different API's were specific to the various
operating systems. I won't pretend to know exactly the differences, but since
all of them were developed, MS has now globbed them all into one, the idea
being that the confusion was hurting them. However, I did understand, generally
why there were so many (there were more than I listed), and think there will
be even more problems now. From what I gather, the consolidations of API's
may actually jeopardize 32-bit Windows application compatability with
Chicago (Win4.0). This may be the reason for the schedule slip. I could go
into it much more. But, the bottom line is, this two-path strategy between
the various operating systems is going to hurt Microsoft, and they now know
this.

> * NT: sure it's big and slow and clumsy, and hardly anybody is
> running it, but so what?  Seems to me that Microsoft knows
> exactly what they're doing -- 1995 or so.  The one thing that
> Microsoft seems to know better than anybody is the pace of
> technology adoption.  The only reason it's out there now is
> so that developers can see what's on the horizon.  If these
> "indicators" are just that a fair number of people forced
> to choose an OS for LAN server machines right now are picking
> OS/2 instead of NT, that doesn't seem very convincing unless
> they're converting the ENTIRE operation to OS/2.  As long
> as they have to deal with some kind of Windows system on
> a regular basis, they'll be perfectly happy to switch to
> NT when appropriate, patting themselves on the back for
> their prudent and timely choices.  For that switching to
> be painful enough to prevent abandoning OS/2, these organizations
> will have to have developed lots of in-house OS/2 code not easily
> portable to NT.  I think most MIS types would call that
> a "mistake".

Well, you are missing what is happening, to a point. First, for those who need
OS's for LAN's and servers, they _ARE_ by and large going with NT. This then
begs the question of where OS/2 is supposedly gaining market share. Well, guess
what, OS/2 is no longer being marketed as a server operating system. It's
gaining market share, and FAST, in the single-user market! As for MIS decisions?
NT is indeed clumsy, and is nowhere near well-positioned for the desktop. So, in
cases where shops NEED 32-bit OS (and that number is increasing exponentially as
the applications demand more power) at the desktop, they are going OS/2. Let us
look at the scorecard:

1. OS/2 runs all existing 16-bit apps faster than Win3.1 or WinNT. Same for DOS
apps. (I hear an upcoming release of NT may help to close the gap, though.)
2. OS/2, new or upgrade, is cheaper, and more efficient. Takes up only 40MB of
disk space vs. 80 I believe. Why? They don't package in everything you don't
need. Good example: desktop users do NOT need SMP capability build in: NT users
pay for it no matter what.
3. OS/2 is potentially 12 months ahead of anything equivalent from MS, maybe
even more. And, IBM marketing types are finally learning how to sell this OS.

> * Windows everywhere:  by the time any new architecture starts
> to gain popularity, some version of Windows will run on it.
> This cross-platform availability seems like a really strong
> strategy to me.

Actually, this whole NT vs. OS/2 war may be a factor in killing any new
architecture hopes (I am obviously in favor of this, working for Intel, but the
point is valid): OS/2 _is_ gaining momentum fast in the already-isntalled base
of x86-architecture machines. An installed base which dwarfs any other
architecture existing. Porting _the_ operating system used on these machines to
other architectures will help to kill the x86 platform (or diminish it). But,
because of OS/2, the jury is still out on what operating system will be used on
the x86 machines (and specifically, the power users and corporate accounts) by
the time other significant architectures are ready to compete (namely, PPC and
Alpha come to mind). I cannot comment on whether or not OS/2 will have
cross-platform interoperability, but I bet market demand will see it happen.
As it is, NT cross-platform availability does little, since NT still only adds
value to the high-end market, which is already comprised of primarily non-x86
machines anyway. For the record, though, OS/2 will be ported to RS/6000, which
is certainly a good member of the RISC market. And, since IBM makes PPC601, we
can only speculate that the PPC is a good candidate as well.

> I believe it's accepted by everybody that any operating system
> will have to run Windows applications to have a chance of gaining
> significant (non-niche) market share.

And it does. The question is: what is Windows applications? Answer: 16-bit.
There simply isn't more than a handful of 32-bit apps out there, certainly not
enough to drive the market any time soon. 16-bit Windows apps are still the
market drivers. Interestingly, though, while it never caught on before, thanks
to IBM's wonderful marketing (sarcasm in the extreme), 32-bit OS/2 apps are now
being made by the dozen. The numbers should easily match NT 32-bit apps month by
month. You can bet Microsoft is worried about these factors...

> Don't get me wrong -- though I'm betting on Windows I don't
> particularly like it.  If I had my choice, I guess I'd pick
> NextStep -- but I don't get to pick which operating environment
> wins.

Well, I have seen NextStep on a 486, and it flat-out crawls (either it was much
better in a real Next box, or Windows did a lot of catching up since Next took
the industry by surprise so many years ago. I think a bit of both). I like NT a
lot, but it doesn't perform as well, and is a resource hog. I like the OS/2
interface slightly less, but it's what I'm going with on my desktop ASAP. As an
applications developer (for 16-bit apps), I have no choice really. Windows 3.1
simply crashed and burns many times daily (and believe me, I'm an expert on
doing everything possible to keep this to a minimum), and NT would slow me down
too much, since nothing I work with is 32-bit at this point (I work with a
number of development tools such as Visual Basic, Power Builder, etc...). I'm
not alone. I see a growing movement of productivity-minded users (word
processing, spreadsheet), also growing increasingly dissatisfied with 3.1, and
faced with almost no choice (truly, you have to compare NT and OS/2 head to head
to see how poorly NT rates).

> How can OS/2 possibly win in an environment like this?  The only
> way seems to be that it'll be a cheaper NT than NT, but I don't
> see how IBM has any hope of diverting the flood of developers
> committing to Windows.

That flood of developers is already starting to shrink, not grow, as they now
realize that NT is never going to hit the desktop in a big way. Note: NT was
always the "great hope". Only late in the development cycle did Microsoft start
admitting that Chicago is their target OS for the desktop, not NT. And, likely
the reason why Chicago is now delayed, many NT apps would not otherwise run on
Chicago until ported! This is because, while it offers many things NT does not,
it is missing many things NT has, threads being a real big one!!! That's right,
Windows 4.0 doesn't do multi-threading, along with a number of other things many
NT developers have been using.

Anyway, the big NT momentum was during 92 and early 93. Now, after release, that
momentum has in _fact_ reversed, and OS/2's is picking up. Now for a little
reality check: the gap is _enormous_! OS/2 has a _lot_ of ground to cover if it
is to truly compete. Were it not for the many misfortunes Microsoft has had from
the start, IBM would have bowed out long ago. Instead, the opportunity has been
presented to IBM, and they're doing everything they can to seize it. Does
Microsoft still have the advantage? Of course. The signs simply indicate that
this advantage is slipping. Just like in a tennis match, however, if you're up
by 3 sets, you can afford to give one up. It only takes one game to win the
match. Microsoft has just been losing every game and set for the last 6 months
is all...


  I know that lots of OS/2 applications
> are being released, but again that doesn't really mean much --
> nearly all of those applications are half-hearted ports of
> programs developed originally for other platforms.

This is true, but true 32-bit NT apps aren't any more signficant at this time.
I've seen a few. No Word or Excel or anything yet. OS/2 users who upgraded to
v2.1 at least now have their old word processors and such in 32-bit form (much
has already been ported), plus all the 16-bit Windows apps available. Another
advantage for Microsoft, though, is their own software suite. Let us not forget
that Microsoft is also the application giant, not just the OS giant. And, while
their own applications aren't yet available for NT (and won't be for a while
yet), they may _never_ be ported to OS/2, simply because of the war waging
between them and IBM. Porting Word, Excel and the others to OS/2 would basically
hand over the game to IBM right there. It's an ace in the hole which Microsoft
is probably tenuously clinging to right now.

As an in-house consultant (quasi-officially for certain departments), I do
suggest OS/2 for desktops wherever there is available support put in place.
And, I see most server decisions moving towards NT. I can't "officially" comment
on any break-downs of what we support and don't here (at Intel) internally, but
I can tell you that I don't _know_ anyone running NT on the desktop for anything
other than evaluation purposes, and I _do_ know plenty of people running OS/2. I
also know, from talking to folks at Microsoft, that they also use a lot of OS/2
there. Even they aren't a homogeneous NT shop.


